The document's front-matter declarations — the metadata elements, including the context-resolved title and subtitle variants that a render or import produces.

<section #l1-meta | `<meta>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `structured-data-containers` · Layer 1 `<meta>` · JATS `<article-meta, book-meta, or book-part-meta>` · `content` `structured`

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `type` | kwarg | `article` `book` `book-part` `website` | Declares the document class. Resolved ONCE before structuring by enscribeDocTypeResolve: the value is validated against this set, stored on file.data, and an explicitly-set unknown type (a typo, an unbuilt class) is reported with a non-fatal diagnostic and falls back to "article" (always-renders). The structural plugins (enscribeArticleStructuring / enscribeBookStructuring) read the resolved class to decide which Layer 1 wrapper to generate around the document: type=article → <article> with <article-front>/<article-body>/<article-back>; type=book → <book> with <book-front>/<book-body>/<book-back>; type=book-part → <book-part> containing <meta> and body content directly (no nested front/body/back wrappers). type=website → no Layer 1 wrapper; enscribeWebsiteStructuring builds a nav model on file.data from the master's <nav> (#246). HTML render only — no JATS/BITS (a site is not a scholarly document). Default is "article" — the most common case. An absent type kwarg is the normal article case: silent default, no diagnostic. |
| `book-part-type` | kwarg | `chapter` `part` `introduction` `conclusion` `other` `preface` `foreword` `dedication` `appendix` `glossary` `colophon` `afterword` | For a single-file book-part (type=book-part) only: sets the book-part-type on the generated `<book-part>` wrapper (#176). This is how a standalone book-part file declares whether it is an appendix, preface, etc. — a single-file appendix needs book-part-type=appendix (#100), so it is not always "chapter". Read by enscribeBookStructuring; allowlisted but not lifted (a structural kwarg, like type — it configures the wrapper, it is not a descriptive `<meta>` field). Default "chapter" when unset. An unknown value is reported with a non-fatal diagnostic and the document still renders (always-renders). Ignored on type=article / type=book documents, which route their book-parts by the `<chapter>` / `<appendix>` / … shorthand instead. |
| `icon` | kwarg |  | Optional brand icon for a website's top bar (#246): the path or URL of the site icon, read by the website chrome (S2). Descriptive metadata, like the title; ignored by article/book documents. The brand NAME is `<meta>`'s title — there is no in-header `<icon>`/`<title>` tag. |

Produced by: canonical `<meta>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code #code:l1-meta-1>
<meta type=article>
  <title | The Effect of Elephants on Climate>
  <author | Jane Goodall>
  <date | 2024-03-15>
</meta>

<section | Introduction>
The paper begins.
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-meta-1>
<meta type=article>
  <title | The Effect of Elephants on Climate>
  <author | Jane Goodall>
  <date | 2024-03-15>
</meta>

<section | Introduction>
The paper begins.
</minipage>

*Author writes &lt;meta type=article&gt; at the top with no &lt;article&gt; wrapper. The structural plugin reads type=article and generates: - the &lt;article&gt; container - &lt;article-front&gt; wrapping the original &lt;meta&gt; - &lt;article-body&gt; wrapping the section content &lt;title&gt; is promoted to &lt;article-title&gt; as the first child of &lt;meta&gt;. &lt;meta&gt; itself survives in the output, inside &lt;article-front&gt;.*

<code #code:l1-meta-2>
<meta type=book>
  <title | A Natural History of Elephants>
  <author | Jane Goodall>
</meta>

<chapter | Origins>
Content.
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-meta-2>
<meta type=book>
  <title | A Natural History of Elephants>
  <author | Jane Goodall>
</meta>

<chapter | Origins>
Content.
</minipage>

*type=book generates the book-shaped wrapper instead. Changing the single kwarg switches the entire output structure. Each book-part contains its own &lt;meta&gt; with &lt;book-part-title&gt;; no &lt;book-part-meta&gt; wrapper.*

</section>

<section #l1-config | `<config>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `configuration` · Layer 1 `<config>` · JATS `<no direct equivalent>` · `content` `none`

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `notes` | kwarg |  |  |

Produced by: canonical `<config>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code #code:l1-config-1>
<config citation-style=author-year number-sections=true />
</code>

*Structural / front-matter — shown as source. It carries metadata or apparatus (configuration, stored data, document front matter), not standalone body content, so there is no in-flow live box.*

*The authoring form for &lt;config&gt; is kwargs. Settings are read at the discovery pass into the configuration registry; the element itself produces no body output (it renders as an empty &lt;config&gt;).*

<code #code:l1-config-2>
<config number-figures=true number-tables=true show-source=true />
</code>

*Structural / front-matter — shown as source. It carries metadata or apparatus (configuration, stored data, document front matter), not standalone body content, so there is no in-flow live box.*

*More operational options, all from the live kwarg allowlist (citation-style, number-sections, number-figures, number-tables, number-equations, show-source, parse-data-tables, ref-prefix-).*

</section>

<section #l1-title | `<title>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `metadata` · Layer 1 `<title>` · JATS `<article-title or book-title (inside title-group inside article-meta or book-meta)>` · `content` `prose` → `<children>`

*No kwargs or boolean flags.*

Produced by: canonical `<title>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

