</meta>
</code>

*Structural / front-matter — shown as source. It carries metadata or apparatus (configuration, stored data, document front matter), not standalone body content, so there is no in-flow live box.*

*Comma-separated keyword list — the simpler and more common authoring form. The exporter splits on commas when emitting JATS.*

<code #code:l1-keywords-2>
<meta keywords="elephants, conservation, climate" />
</code>

*Structural / front-matter — shown as source. It carries metadata or apparatus (configuration, stored data, document front matter), not standalone body content, so there is no in-flow live box.*

*Kwarg-form authoring lifts to the child-tag form at the gate.*

</section>

<section #l1-subject | `<subject>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `metadata` · Layer 1 `<subject>` · JATS `<subject>` · `content` `prose` → `<children>`

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `scheme` | kwarg |  | Optional classification scheme this subject belongs to (e.g. "MSC2020" for the AMS Mathematics Subject Classification, "ACM" for ACM Computing Classification, "MeSH" for biomedical subjects). Identifies the controlled vocabulary the subject value is drawn from. |

Produced by: canonical `<subject>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code #code:l1-subject-1>
<meta>
  <subject | Ecology of large mammals>
</meta>
</code>

*Structural / front-matter — shown as source. It carries metadata or apparatus (configuration, stored data, document front matter), not standalone body content, so there is no in-flow live box.*

*Free-form subject. Common for general-interest documents.*

<code #code:l1-subject-2>
<meta>
  <subject scheme=MSC2020 | 92D40>
  <subject scheme=MSC2020 | 92D25>
</meta>
</code>

*Structural / front-matter — shown as source. It carries metadata or apparatus (configuration, stored data, document front matter), not standalone body content, so there is no in-flow live box.*

*Multiple subjects from a controlled vocabulary. The scheme identifies the classification system; the JATS exporter generates the appropriate &lt;subj-group subj-group-type="..."&gt; wrapper.*

</section>

<section #l1-author | `<author>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `structured-data-containers` · Layer 1 `<author>` · JATS `<contrib contrib-type="author">` · `content` `structured`

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `name` | kwarg |  | The author's name. Authored as a kwarg, lifted at the normalize-to-canonical gate to a <name> child tag. Equivalent to authoring <name \| ...> inside the <author>'s child-tag form. |
| `affiliation` | kwarg |  | Author's institutional affiliation. Lifts to <affiliation>. |
| `orcid` | kwarg |  | Author's ORCID identifier (e.g., 0000-0000-0000-0000). Lifts to <orcid>. |
| `email` | kwarg |  | Author's contact email address. Lifts to <email>. |
| `corresponding` | kwarg | `true` `false` | Marks this author as the corresponding author (JATS contrib corresp="yes"). A scalar marker — stays as a kwarg/ attribute on the canonical Layer 1 <author>; never lifted to a child tag. Both surface forms are accepted: +corresponding (boolean shorthand) and corresponding=true (explicit kwarg) both normalize to a `corresponding="true"` attribute on the canonical Layer 1 node. The structured-element gate promotes the +form into the kwarg surface so the schema renderer's attribute mapping fires uniformly. |

Produced by: canonical `<author>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code #code:l1-author-1>
<author | Jane Goodall>
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-author-1>
<author | Jane Goodall>
</minipage>

*Backward-compatible casual form (carried forward from before the structured-interface reconciliation). The pipe content sits as text content of &lt;author&gt;; it is NOT lifted to a &lt;name&gt; child — authors who want the structured shape use the kwarg form below or the child-tag form. JATS export reads the name string either way.*

<code #code:l1-author-2>
<author name="Jane Goodall" orcid="0000-0001-2345-6789" affiliation="Cambridge University" +corresponding />
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-author-2>
<author name="Jane Goodall" orcid="0000-0001-2345-6789" affiliation="Cambridge University" +corresponding />
</minipage>

*Kwarg form. Each lifted kwarg becomes a child tag at the gate; +corresponding stays as a boolean kwarg on the canonical Layer 1 &lt;author&gt;.*

<code #code:l1-author-3>
<author +corresponding>
  <name | Jane Goodall>
  <affiliation | Cambridge University>
  <orcid | 0000-0001-2345-6789>
  <email | jane@example.com>
</author>
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-author-3>
<author +corresponding>
  <name | Jane Goodall>
  <affiliation | Cambridge University>
  <orcid | 0000-0001-2345-6789>
  <email | jane@example.com>
</author>
</minipage>

*Child-tag form. The canonical Layer 1 shape. Both this form and the equivalent kwarg form above produce the same Layer 1 output.*

<code #code:l1-author-4>
<meta>
  <author | Jane Goodall>
  <author | David Attenborough>
  <author +corresponding | Charles Darwin>
</meta>
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-author-4>
<meta>
  <author | Jane Goodall>
  <author | David Attenborough>
  <author +corresponding | Charles Darwin>
</meta>
</minipage>

*Multiple authors are sibling &lt;author&gt; elements inside &lt;meta&gt;. The third is the corresponding author. JATS export groups them as &lt;contrib-group&gt;.*

</section>

